  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "get_num",
    "file": "mruby/mrbgems/mruby-sprintf/src/sprintf.c",
    "description": "`get_num` parses a format width/precision into the wider `mrb_int n`, but then narrows it to `int` with `*valp = (int)n` without checking `n <= INT_MAX`. A large but valid `mrb_int` precision therefore wraps negative. In `mrb_str_format`'s `%s` path, that negative precision passes `prec < slen`, is used for an invalid pointer offset, and becomes the negative `len` passed through `PUSH` to `memcpy`; `CHECK` also assumes the length is nonnegative and does not reject it. The patch should reject parsed numeric specifiers outside the destination `int` range before assignment (and preferably defensively reject negative copy/fill lengths).",
    "conditions": "The fuzzer input is parsed as Ruby source using byte 0x10 as a percent-string delimiter: `%\\x10%.11111111111s\\x10%9` executes essentially `\"%.11111111111s\" % 9`. The format precision `11111111111` fits the build's 64-bit `mrb_int` but not 32-bit `int`; narrowing wraps it to a negative value (observed approximately -1773791113). Formatting argument `9` as string `\"9\"` then uses that negative precision as the substring length, and `memcpy` receives the resulting huge unsigned size. Any `%s` conversion with an inline decimal precision that fits `mrb_int` but narrows to a negative `int`, plus a convertible argument, can reach this crash."
  },
